# rules: csharp-stream-never-closed
# languages: c_sharp
# fixture: csharp/CWE-772.cs
# from: CWE-772 (Missing Release of Resource), CWE-404
#
# A stream holds an operating system handle. The garbage collector will run the
# finalizer eventually and the handle will close eventually, and neither of
# those is a schedule: on Windows the file stays locked until it happens, so
# the next write fails with a sharing violation rather than anything that names
# the leak. Under load the process runs out of handles instead. Both symptoms
# appear a long way from this line.
#
# C# has one right answer and it is syntax, which makes this rule unusually
# clean to write. `using (var s = ...)` and, since C# 8, `using var s = ...`
# both dispose at the end of the scope whatever happens in between, exceptions
# included. So the rule is: a stream was constructed in a declaration that is
# not a `using`, and nothing disposed it.
#
# Three things stop it firing, and each is a real way of being correct:
#
#   - the declaration is a `using`, in either spelling.
#   - `Dispose` or `Close` is called on the name somewhere in the method.
#   - the method's return type is a stream. Then whatever it opens is the
#     caller's to close, and the method is a factory rather than a leak.
#     Reporting those is how a resource rule gets a reputation for crying wolf,
#     because every `OpenRead` helper in every codebase looks like this.
#     Reading the return type rather than looking for `return s;` in the body
#     matters: the body test would have to be a regex, `return\s+\w+;` matches
#     `return null;` and `return true;` as well, and those are common enough
#     that the rule would have gone quiet nearly everywhere.
#
# Two of those three are asked of the method's text rather than of its shape,
# and the reason is a C# limitation worth stating. The C# 8 using declaration -
# `using var r = new StreamReader(path);` - has no reading: written as a
# pattern the grammar drops the `using` and matches every declaration in the
# file, so the matcher refuses it rather than answering wrongly. There is
# therefore no way to say "this declaration is a using" structurally, and the
# same goes for "this method returns the name this declaration made", which in
# Java is one pattern with two holes and `where_same` and here is a
# multi-statement pattern, which C# has no reading for either.
#
# So a method containing any `using` at all, a `.Dispose(` or a `.Close(` is
# left alone entirely. The `using` half has to be that broad because C# spells
# it four ways and only one of them has a reading: `using (x) {}`, `using var
# x = ...`, `using FileStream x = ...`, and `await using` in front of any of
# those. ShareX and Jellyfin between them used three of the four, and a rule
# that knew only `using var` reported all three as leaks. That is coarser than it should be: a
# method that closes one stream and leaks another goes unreported. It errs the
# quiet way on purpose - a resource rule that reports a factory method is one
# people turn off - and the cost is written here rather than hidden.
#
# What it also cannot see is a field. A stream assigned to `this._stream` and closed
# in `Dispose()` is the correct implementation of IDisposable and is not
# reported, because the constructor is not a `using` and the disposal is in
# another method - so the rule is confined to declarations inside a method
# body, which is where the leaks actually are.

["$RT $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }"] as $methods
| ["$T $V = new $S($$$_);"] as $opened
| ["using ($T $V = new $S($$$_)) { $$$_ }", "using $T $V = new $S($$$_);"] as $scoped
| "\\.\\s*(?:Dispose|DisposeAsync|Close)\\s*\\(|\\busing\\s*[\\(A-Za-z_]" as $disposes
| "(?:Stream|Reader|Writer|Connection|Client|MemoryMappedFile)$" as $isAStreamType
| "^(?:FileStream|StreamWriter|StreamReader|BinaryWriter|BinaryReader|MemoryMappedFile|NamedPipeClientStream|NamedPipeServerStream|SqlConnection|HttpClient|TcpClient|UdpClient|SocketStream|GZipStream|DeflateStream|CryptoStream)$" as $isAStream
| scan_ast("*.cs"; $methods + $opened + $scoped) as $all
| ($all | of($opened) | where_capture("S"; $isAStream))
| within($all | of($methods))
| outside($all | of($scoped))
| outside($all | of($methods) | where_capture("BODY"; $disposes))
| outside($all | of($methods) | where_capture("RT"; $isAStreamType))
| where_capture_not("V"; "^_")
| finding("csharp-stream-never-closed";
    "this stream is never disposed, so the handle stays open until the "
    + "finalizer runs - on Windows the file stays locked until then, and "
    + "under load the process runs out of handles first")
| report
